.TH DISKC 1 "November 13, 2026" "diskc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diskc \- explain filesystem pressure, large files, growth, and writers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diskc
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B diskc light
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B diskc list
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B diskc db
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B diskc docker
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B diskc doc
.RI [ path ]
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B diskc mac system
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B diskc mac user
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B diskc version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diskc
is a read-only disk troubleshooting command for Linux and macOS. It reports
filesystem and inode pressure, large allocated files and directories, observed
growth, active Linux writers, and deleted-but-open Linux files.
.PP
Without a path, diskc inspects the root filesystem. Multiple paths share one
growth-sampling window. Explicit paths are the opt-in mechanism for network
filesystems; automatic all-filesystem scans exclude remote mounts.
.PP
Symbolic links in a scan root are resolved before scanning. File and directory
paths use the resolved target; the filesystem summary keeps the requested path.
For example, diskc light /var on macOS scans /private/var. Symbolic links
encountered inside the scanned tree are skipped; pass linked data directories
as separate roots to inspect them. Broken or looping root links produce an
inspection error.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B light
Use a shallow scan, report ten entries, disable growth waiting, and skip process
file-descriptor inspection.
.TP
.B list
List mounted filesystems without traversing files. Remote and unknown mounts are
identified but are not probed, avoiding hangs on stale network mounts.
.TP
.B db
Discover common PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, Redis, ClickHouse, and MongoDB paths
and inspect their disk usage without connecting to a database.
.TP
.B docker
Inspect common Docker, containerd, BuildKit, and rootless storage locations
without requiring Docker daemon access.
.TP
.B doc
Show diskc's built-in defaults, build information, mount discovery, filesystem
access, capacity and inode pressure, and process inspection capability. The
default path is /; use an explicit path to check another filesystem. No file
tree is scanned. --json emits a machine-readable report.
.TP
.B mac system
Inspect common macOS system and user storage roots and provide conservative
cleanup guidance. Nothing is deleted.
.TP
.B mac user
Inspect selected storage directories in the current Mac user's home: caches,
logs, application support, sandbox containers, developer data, downloads, and
Trash. Defaults: --top 20 --depth 5 --sample 0. Nothing is deleted or emptied.
.TP
.B version
Print version, commit, build date, Go version, and target platform.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --top " N"
Number of largest and fastest-growing files to report. Default: 20.
.TP
.BI --depth " N"
Maximum directory depth to scan. Default: 4.
Depth is relative to the resolved root: 0 includes files directly in the root;
1 also includes files in its immediate child directories.
.TP
.BI --sample " DURATION"
Growth measurement window. Default: 3s. Use 0 to disable.
.PP
Filesystem growth and time-to-full use net available-byte loss during this
window, including activity outside scanned candidates. Per-file growth is
attribution only; zero or negative net growth has no time-to-full estimate.
.PP
File growth requires the same regular file (device and inode) at the scan and
both sample observations. Replaced files have their current regular-file size
refreshed but no measured rate; resample to measure the new file. Unavailable
or non-regular paths retain their last observed regular-file size and have no
measured rate. Replacement symlinks are not followed. These files are labeled
in text and excluded from the fastest-growth ranking in all scan commands.
.TP
.B --all
Inspect all automatically discovered local filesystems.
.TP
.B --watch
Refresh continuously. Terminal output is redrawn only when stdout is a terminal.
.TP
.BI --interval " DURATION"
Delay after each completed watch scan. Default: 3s.
.TP
.B --json
Emit JSON. With --watch, emit one compact JSON value per line (NDJSON).
Sampled files include growth_status: measured, replaced, or unavailable.
The field is omitted when sampling is disabled. A zero rate for replaced or
unavailable files is a placeholder, not measured zero growth; macOS system and
user JSON reports omit zero rate values.
.SH SIZE SEMANTICS
File and directory rankings use filesystem-allocated bytes rather than apparent
length. Sparse files therefore do not look like consumed disk space, and hard
links are counted once per scan. Normal, light, database, Docker, and macOS
system text reports append [N apparent] to file size rows when the positive
apparent length differs from allocated bytes. Existing directory size rows use
the same annotation. Equal sizes and zero apparent lengths have no annotation.
JSON retains both allocated_bytes and apparent_bytes.
.PP
For example, 4.0 KB /data/sparse.img [1.0 GB apparent] describes a file with a
1 GB logical length that currently consumes 4 KB of allocated blocks. Apparent
length can also be smaller than allocated space, such as for a small file
occupying a full block. The annotation does not identify why the sizes differ;
use allocated bytes to investigate current disk consumption.
.PP
Filesystem output distinguishes used, free, available, reserved, and pressure.
Pressure is based on bytes unavailable to an unprivileged process.
.PP
File growth is net allocated-block change, not total write throughput.
Truncation and regrowth of the same inode between observations can be missed.
Directory totals remain the initial scan snapshot even when sampled file sizes
are refreshed.
.PP
When a scan exceeds the bounded directory tracker, affected directory rows are
labeled (estimated) in normal, light, Docker, and macOS system and user text
reports.
JSON directory objects include estimated: true for these rows; the field is
omitted for non-estimated rows. A scan warning also identifies bounded
heavy-hitter estimates, though detailed warnings can be capped.
.PP
Estimated counters can include values carried over from an evicted directory;
they are investigation leads rather than measured totals for that path.
Inspect a reported subtree as an explicit scan root with an appropriate --depth
to narrow the investigation, for example
diskc light /var/lib/docker/overlay2 --depth 4.
A large subtree can still exceed the tracker. Rows without the estimate marker
remain subject to the scan-scope limits below.
.SH SCAN SCOPE
Every successful normal, light, database, Docker, and macOS system scan reports
the resolved root, selected maximum directory depth, unique regular files
observed before candidate pruning, directories skipped by depth, filesystem
device, or pseudo-filesystem path rules, skipped symbolic links, and inspection
errors. Directory totals cover observed files only; they exclude directory
metadata, other filesystems, deleted-open files, and unobserved contents.
.PP
Counters cover boundaries actually encountered, not descendants hidden behind
them. A directory is counted under its first applicable exclusion:
pseudo-filesystem, depth, then filesystem device. Links in skipped subtrees are
not counted. Inspection errors are counted even when detailed warnings are
capped. Zero counts do not establish complete filesystem visibility.
.PP
If depth hides subtrees, increase --depth or inspect a relevant subtree
explicitly, for example diskc light /data/service --depth 4. Inspect linked or
mounted data roots explicitly when needed; a remote path can block on a stale
mount. For inspection errors, review warnings and access. All checks are
read-only.
.PP
JSON includes scan_scope beside filesystem in normal/light reports, on each
successful database filesystems entry, Docker paths entry, or macOS roots entry.
Failed scans do not claim a successful scope. Fields are root, max_depth,
regular_files, skipped_depth_directories, skipped_filesystem_directories,
skipped_pseudo_directories, skipped_symlinks, and inspection_errors.
All counters
are present, including zeros. Scope describes the initial traversal, before
candidate selection and growth sampling; regular_files can exceed the number
of reported or sampled files.
.SH DOCKER CONFIGURATION VISIBILITY
If a checked daemon.json cannot be read or parsed, text and top-level JSON
warnings include the configuration path and failure reason. Discovery continues
with later configuration candidates or conventional storage paths, but the
actual daemon data root may remain undiscovered. Verify the daemon configuration
and access before treating these paths as complete incident evidence.
Missing optional configuration files are skipped quietly. Discovery still stops
at the first successfully read configuration.
.SH MACOS ROOT VISIBILITY
The mac system command keeps a root whose metadata, filesystem usage, or scan
cannot be inspected. Text marks it Skipped with the failure reason and a next
step to review the error and access; a warning also records the failure.
JSON includes skipped: true and skip_reason on that root, without scan_scope
or file rankings. Optional roots absent at the initial metadata check are
omitted quietly. A root that disappears at a later stage is reported as skipped.
.PP
Traversal errors within an otherwise successful scan preserve observed files,
scan_scope.inspection_errors, and warnings. When no regular files were observed
and inspection errors occurred, text says that inspection errors may hide files.
Other roots continue normally. The report describes current-user visibility,
not complete macOS storage usage.
.SH MACOS USER STORAGE
The mac user command checks Library/Caches, Library/Logs,
Library/Application Support, Library/Containers, Library/Group Containers,
Library/Developer, Downloads, and .Trash under the current home directory.
It requires macOS and a valid absolute home directory. Each directory is an
explicit scan root; --depth is relative to that location. Optional growth
sampling uses one shared window.
.PP
Application support and sandbox container data retain protective guidance even
under nested cache or log directories. Developer storage can include projects,
archives, simulator data, and build products; review it in the owning tool.
Missing optional roots are omitted quietly. Failed and partial inspections
retain the skipped-root and scan-scope evidence described above.
.PP
JSON includes scope: "user" and home, plus platform, roots, and optional
warnings.
Results cover selected visible directories, not the whole home, macOS Storage
settings totals, APFS snapshots, or purgeable space. Root symlinks are resolved;
linked or shared content can overlap across roots. Do not sum root results as
unique home usage.
.SH WRITER ATTRIBUTION
On Linux, writable file descriptors are matched to each reported file's last
observed device and inode, including when sampling is disabled. Renaming or
replacing the pathname after observation does not attach the replacement file's
processes to earlier evidence. Paths are historical labels and may have moved.
Overlapping scans retain separate observations; each matching PID appears once
per file even if it holds multiple writable descriptors.
.PP
A writable descriptor is an investigation lead, not proof that a process caused
the sampled growth. Verify the PID, descriptor, and service before taking
action.
Permissions and process exits can hide matches. An absent JSON writers field
means no matching process was visible at inspection, not that no writer exists.
.PP
Deleted-open inspection checks that a descriptor labeled " (deleted)" refers
to a regular file with zero remaining hard links. Linked files whose names
end in that text, and inodes still reachable through another hard link, are
excluded from retained-space results. Device, inode, allocated bytes, and
apparent length come from one stat observation; each inode is counted once.
Read-only descriptors can retain space, so listed processes are holders,
not necessarily writers. Only the final kernel marker is removed from the
reported path, preserving a literal " (deleted)" in the original filename.
Paths are historical labels; verify the PID, descriptor, and service before
planning any action.
.PP
If /proc cannot be read or process descriptor directories or records cannot be
inspected, text and JSON warnings mark writer attribution or deleted-open
inspection as unavailable or partial. Visible matches and retained files are
still reported; retained-space results may be incomplete. Database and Docker
reports include writer-attribution warnings on inspected paths. Normal process
and descriptor disappearance is skipped without a warning.
.PP
Run as the affected service user when practical and verify process visibility.
Processes hidden entirely by /proc policy cannot be counted; no warning does
not establish full visibility. Light mode skips these checks, and macOS does
not perform Linux process inspection.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates success or help, 1 indicates a runtime failure, and 2 indicates
invalid command usage. Broken-pipe termination is treated as success.
.SH MACOS PACKAGES
From a source checkout with Go 1.22 or newer, run
sh scripts/build-macos.sh to build Apple Silicon (arm64) and Intel (amd64)
archives plus checksums.txt in dist/macos. An optional output directory is the
script's only argument. Existing package and checksum files are not overwritten.
.PP
On the destination Mac, verify the packages with shasum -a 256 -c checksums.txt,
then extract the matching archive. Run ./diskc version, ./diskc list,
\&./diskc light "$HOME", or ./diskc mac system --depth 2 --top 10 in Terminal.
The destination does not need Go or root access. Local packages are not
Developer ID signed or notarized. Linux /proc writer attribution and
deleted-open-file discovery are unavailable on macOS.
.SH PERMISSIONS
No root access is required. Files and Linux process details hidden by
permissions cannot be reported; run diskc as the affected service user when
practical.
.SH SAFETY
diskc is diagnostic and read-only. Validate findings before deleting files or
taking production action. See DISCLAIMER.md in the distribution.
.SH AUTHORS
diskc contributors.
